<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="prep-2-8">
  <title>Resources for 2/8</title>

  <objectives>
    <introduction>
      <p>For the content of this class, you will be expected to be able to do the following:</p>
    </introduction>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Explain the definition of an <em>equivalence class</em> of a relatio
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Explain how only equivalence relations give rise to equivalence classes.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Prove theorems about equivalence relations and equivalence classes.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <subsection xml:id="video-activity-equivalence-class-thm">
    <title>Video &amp; Activities for Equivalence Classes - Theorems</title>
    <p>
      Below is a YouTube video on <em>equivalence classes</em>. It is followed by a link to a set of activities that we will be working on in class. Please watch the video and do as many of the activities as you can before class.
    </p>

    <video youtube="IZzOgiYB4o4" />

    <problem>
      <title>Equivalence Class Theorems</title>
      <p>
        Let the relation <m>R</m> on <m>A</m> (i.e. from <m>A</m> to <m>A</m>) be an equivalence relation.  As before, for each <m>b \in A</m>, let <m>R[b] = \{ a \in A : aRb \}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Prove that <m>b \in R[b]</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Prove that for all <m>x \in R[b]</m>, we have <m>xRb</m> and <m>bRx</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Prove that for all <m>x,y \in R[b]</m>, <m>xRy</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Prove that if <m>xRy</m> and <m>a \in R[x]</m>, then <m>a \in R[y]</m>.  Similarly, prove that if <m>a \in R[y]</m>, then <m>a \in R[x]</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Use what you proved above to prove that <m>R[x] = R[y]</m> if and only if <m>xRy</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Suppose that <m>a \in R[b] \cap R[c]</m>.  What can you say about <m>R[a]</m>, <m>R[b]</m>, and <m>R[c]</m>?  Prove it.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Let <m>R[b_1]</m>, <m>R[b_2], \ldots , R[b_r]</m> be all the distinct <m>R[b]</m>'s (i.e. all the distinct families).  If <m>a \in A</m>, prove that <m>a \in R[b_1] \cup R[b_2] \cup \cdots \cup R[b_r]</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </problem>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Optional Reading</title>
    <p>
      Section 7.3 (Sundstrom): pp. 387-396
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>